## Medicines

Most people take a combination of medicines, such as:

| Medicine | What it does |
| --- | --- |
| **Aspirin** or another antiplatelet | Makes blood less sticky, lowering the risk of clots |
| **Statins**, such as atorvastatin | Lower cholesterol |
| **Beta blockers**, such as bisoprolol | Slow the heart and ease angina |
| **Calcium channel blockers** | Relax the arteries and ease angina |
| **ACE inhibitors**, such as ramipril | Lower blood pressure and protect the heart |
| **GTN spray or tablets** | Quickly relieve an angina attack |

Keep taking your medicines even when you feel well, and talk to your GP or pharmacist about any side effects.

{% callout type="warn" title="Using a GTN spray for angina" %}
1. **Stop and sit down.**
2. Use your **GTN spray** under your tongue.
3. If the pain has not gone after **5 minutes**, use it again.
4. If the pain has not gone **5 minutes after the second dose**, **call 999**.
{% /callout %}

## Procedures

If medicines do not control your symptoms, or tests show a high risk, you may be offered:

- **coronary angioplasty and stent (PCI):** a small balloon is passed into the narrowed artery through a blood vessel in your wrist or groin, and a mesh tube called a stent keeps it open
- **coronary artery bypass graft (CABG):** open heart surgery where a blood vessel from elsewhere in your body is used to bypass the narrowed artery

## Cardiac rehabilitation

After a heart attack, stent or heart surgery, you should be offered **cardiac rehabilitation**. It is a free programme of exercise, education and support, often in groups or online. It helps you recover, gain confidence and lower your risk of another heart problem. **It makes a real difference, so take up your place if you are offered one.**

## Other conditions linked to CHD

Coronary heart disease can lead to **heart failure** (when the heart does not pump as well as it should) or **abnormal heart rhythms**. These have their own treatments. Tell your GP if you notice more breathlessness, swollen ankles or palpitations.

Next, read about [living well with coronary heart disease](/conditions/heart-and-circulation/coronary-heart-disease/living-well/).
